WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.140
ED GLAESER: Venice is today an urban Disneyland,

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:06.740
a place of remarkable beauty, but little economic dynamism beyond the tourist

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:07.550
trade.

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:11.780
The historic center of the city welcomed 2.6 million tourists in 2014

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:15.320
but housed only 60,000 permanent residents, less than one half

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:18.800
of its population in either the 1960s or the 1600s.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.440
Yet, the city's beauty reflects centuries of greatness.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.330
For 600 years, Venice was among the most prosperous places on the planet.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:31.280
The city's great wealth was produced primarily by trade, not conquest,

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.330
although it would be a mistake to think of the Lion of Venice as a tame beast.

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:39.080
Venetian commercial success also rested on perhaps the most effective

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.650
urban government of the age, which could maintain internal order,

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:47.000
deliver basic sanitary services, and fight wars with far mightier neighbors

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.390
in order to defend the city's vital trade routes.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:53.060
The history of Venice begins in the chaotic centuries

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.340
after the fall of the Western Roman Empire.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:58.640
The islands of the Venetian Lagoon were defensible space protected

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:00.890
against marauding Goths and Lombards.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:03.254
Even the seaward approach to Venice is hazardous.

00:01:03.254 --> 00:01:06.170
And the Venetians would later protect themselves against naval assault

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:08.780
by removing the markers that showed their own ships

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:11.120
the safe path to the city.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.510
The Venetian community was supposedly started as a trading

00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:17.530
outpost of nearby Padua in 421.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:20.840
In 528, Cassiodorus wrote of the settlement's abundance

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.880
of fish and its busy saltworks.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.340
Venice's urban edge, safety from attack, became even more important

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.160
when the Lombards conquered the Byzantine Empire's outpost

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:35.900
on the Italian mainland, the Exarchate of Ravenna in 751.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:39.650
The islands of the Venetian Lagoon remain free from landlubbing marauders.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:43.580
And refugees came to the last Italian outpost of the Roman Empire.

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:45.500
The leaders of Venice, the doges--

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.960
the word is a variant of the Latin dux or duke--

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:50.630
were originally seen as representatives of the distant emperor

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:52.310
in Constantinople.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:55.400
But by 803, Venice declared itself independent,

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:58.790
enjoying its position as a free port, perfectly placed

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:00.830
between the northern empire of Charlemagne

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:02.960
and the eastern empire of Byzantium.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:06.770
Originally, the island's political core was on the barrier island, the Lido,

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:08.570
now famous for its beach.

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:11.600
But in 810, when the Franks captured that outer island,

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.890
the Doge fled behind the barrier to the area near the Rialto Bridge, which

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:19.340
is protected by water from the shore and protected by the Lido

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:21.610
from the Adriatic Sea.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:26.930
Venice's physical geography, all those canals, delight millions of tourists.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:30.470
But initially, the central core seems to have been a set of marshy islands.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:34.220
The current city is essentially manmade, held up by wooden poles

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.380
and connected by scores of bridges.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:41.060
Venice's long-run success reflected its extraordinary political institutions,

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.130
probably the strongest in the world, which

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:46.700
were capable of harnessing collective action for the common good but also

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:50.670
capable of stopping any one Venetian from establishing a dictatorship.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:54.230
This commercial city was no bastion of laissez faire capitalism.

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:56.690
Arguably, Venice needed such a strong state

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:01.430
because its very geography needed to be transformed to be habitable, safe,

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:03.590
and functional as a port.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:08.090
Even the earliest settlers of Venice seemed to put their houses on stilts

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:09.650
to protect them from the water.

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.990
Cassiodorus describes how Venetian homes lie

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:15.710
like seabirds' nests, half on sea and half on land.

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:18.470
The Doge, Agnello Participazio, who led the defense

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:22.280
against the Frankish attack of 810, set off a major building campaign

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:23.780
on the inner islands.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.660
The Venetians built wooden bridges to connect the low-lying islands.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:29.150
They reinforced banks with sand to protect their settlement

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:29.960
from the waves.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.240
And they drove vast numbers of poles into the water

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:34.730
to provide firm support for their buildings.

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:38.390
Santa Maria della Salute, built in 1630 to thank the Virgin

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:40.970
for freeing the city from the plague, sits atop more than one

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:42.650
million wooden poles.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:45.320
The city built to protect itself from the water.

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:48.020
And it built to enable itself to cross the waves.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:50.810
The Venetian Arsenal was a massive industrial complex

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:53.240
dedicated, above all, to shipbuilding.

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:56.180
It was a public, not a private enterprise.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:58.850
The state would build the ships and then auction them off.

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:01.670
Groups of merchants would buy not the galleys, but the right

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:03.860
to use the galleys for a year.

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:08.429
Central public production enabled the Arsenal to operate on a grand scale

00:04:08.429 --> 00:04:10.220
and to reap the benefits of mass production

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:14.240
six centuries before Henry Ford's cars were made in Detroit.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:16.339
The Arsenal was a public enterprise.

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:18.320
But the Venetian government operated neither

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:22.430
like a modern democracy that tries to balance the needs of all its citizens,

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:24.950
nor like a medieval monarchy fighting to advance

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:28.130
the glory and power of the sovereign and the sovereign's house.

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:30.710
The Venetian Republic was almost like a commercial enterprise

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:34.040
wrapped up in the garments of statecraft.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:37.100
It was run by the Lagoon's leading families, who were themselves

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.990
almost entirely merchants.

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:40.790
And it embarked on undertakings that would

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:43.860
increase the wealth of their trading aristocracy.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:47.180
Did Venice's trading galleys need posts for resupplying themselves

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:48.380
along the Adriatic?

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.884
Did Venice's voracious appetite for wood,

00:04:50.884 --> 00:04:54.050
which was used both to build the city and to build its galley, need forests?

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:56.550
Then the most serene republic would need to conquer outposts

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.045
along the Dalmatian coast of the Adriatic sea.

00:04:59.045 --> 00:05:01.910
Did Venice need a saintly protector who would watch over the ships

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:03.890
and also track the pilgrimage trade?

00:05:03.890 --> 00:05:06.350
Well, then the doge would send merchants to steal

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:10.490
the body of Saint Mark that now lies in the center of the city's cathedral.

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:13.025
The Doge's Palace, a tourist highlight of the city,

00:05:13.025 --> 00:05:15.900
provides a physical tour of the political infrastructure that produce

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:17.870
such a model of corporate governance.

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:19.910
The palace is not the mansion of a prince,

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:23.030
for doges were elected officials who typically took office late in life,

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:25.520
giving hope to us all, but the administrative hub

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:27.230
of a mercantile empire.

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:30.616
The palace contains a glorious Tintoretto portrait of notaries.

00:05:30.616 --> 00:05:32.990
Where else but in Venice would you see such a celebration

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:34.920
of formal financial transactions?

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:38.750
In the massive and magnificently decorated chamber of the Great Council,

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:41.120
all of the adult males of the city's leading families

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:45.110
would assemble to pass laws and to elect the powerful Council of Ten.

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:48.260
The chamber contains portraits of all the city's doges,

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:52.400
except for Marin Falier, whose space is empty.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.240
He tried to take down the republic and bear the palm alone.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:58.730
But unlike Julius Caesar, he failed.

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:00.590
The Venetian Republic's mercantile defenders

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:02.548
were much stronger than the senators who wanted

00:06:02.548 --> 00:06:04.100
to preserve the older Roman Republic.

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:07.355
The Rialto Bridge marks the center of the city's old harbor.

00:06:07.355 --> 00:06:09.770
Galleys would come to the Grand Canal and unload

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:13.430
the riches of the East, spice and silk, gems and glass

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:15.770
brought from Egypt in Constantinople.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:20.000
But some of Venice's Byzantine relics were taken by conquest,

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:21.740
rather than earned through trade.

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:25.430
When Venice sold its services to the soldiers of the Fourth Crusade,

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:28.970
it first demanded military support, help capturing the city of Zara

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:31.760
to provide another trading outpost for the republic.

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:35.090
The pope excommunicated Venice for turning a crusade

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:37.250
into an attack on a Christian city.

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:40.550
The republic regularly shrugged off such displays of papal pique.

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:44.150
Even more importantly, the Venetians and crusaders later repeated this trick

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:46.580
by capturing Constantinople itself.

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:49.040
The horses of the Basilica of San Marco were

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:52.340
stolen from Constantinople's ancient Hippodrome.

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:55.160
Byzantium skilled glass workers were brought back to Venice

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:58.370
and formed the nucleus of the city's remarkable glass industry

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:01.430
that persists to this day, supplying jewelry and knickknacks

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:03.470
for tourists from all over the world.

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:05.540
Later, scholarly refugees from Constantinople

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:07.890
would provide the text for Aldus Manutius,

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:11.850
whose Aldine Press would turn Venice into Europe's printing powerhouse.

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:15.050
Trade and warfare were never far apart during the Middle Ages.

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:18.560
Venice fought bitter wars with Genoa during the 14th century over access

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:20.150
to the markets of the East.

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:22.640
Venice encouraged the Ottomans to snuff out

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:26.270
Portuguese explorers who had the temerity to round the tip of Africa

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:29.450
and obtain direct access to the spice markets of the East.

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:32.600
Venice played an important role in the 1571 Battle of Lepanto,

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:35.900
which stopped Ottoman expansion in the Mediterranean.

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:38.687
The Mediterranean Sea was the highway of Venetian commerce.

00:07:38.687 --> 00:07:40.520
And the republic was always willing to fight

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:43.760
to ensure that its galleys could cross the sea's waters.

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:48.350
As a Mediterranean power, Venice steadily lost importance as commerce

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:49.790
became global.

00:07:49.790 --> 00:07:52.460
Atlantic powers like Holland and England ultimately

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:56.000
became more important mercantile powers during the more globalized

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:57.620
17th century.

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:01.580
Venice's independence was snuffed out by Napoleon in 1797.

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:06.290
But by then, the glory years of the most serene republic were long gone.

00:08:06.290 --> 00:08:11.030
Yet, Venice remains a place of spectacular beauty, built not by kings

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:13.460
but by extremely successful merchants.

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:16.130
It is a city of commerce, but one that reminds

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:19.130
us that commerce, like successful cities,

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:23.260
often rests on the strength of strong political institutions.

